MEASURED ON PRODUCTION, AND THE REASON THIS EXISTS

  QuoteRequest   16 rows   14 PENDING, 0 priced, 0 issued
                           oldest 85 days, newest 2 days
  FunnelEvent     0 rows
  designId        0 rows

DESK-01 put that queue on the dashboard oldest-first, which makes the
backlog visible to whoever opens the dashboard. This is the step before:
whether anybody is told to open it.

THE SILENT FAILURE

lib/email/index.ts picks its transport from the environment — RESEND_API_KEY,
then SMTP_HOST, then 'dev'. In 'dev' it console.logs the message and RETURNS.
It does not throw.

So on a deployment with neither variable set, sendAdminNewQuoteEmail()
RESOLVES SUCCESSFULLY. The `.catch()` that every call site carefully
attaches never fires. `lead.email_failed` is never logged. The visitor is
thanked, the row lands in the database, the request completes exactly as it
does when the mail was really delivered, and there is no error anywhere in
the system. Nobody has been told.

SALE-04's SMS is inert the same way and by design — alertConfig() returns
null without all four Twilio variables and sendLeadAlert() returns — which
is right for an optional channel and equally indistinguishable from success.

Between them those two are the only things that turn a row in a table into
a phone call, and neither of them can fail loudly. Fourteen pending requests
is consistent with that; it does not prove it, and this does not claim it
does. It makes the thing checkable instead of assumed.

WHAT LANDS

lib/delivery-health.ts reads the environment exactly as both senders read
it — same variables, same trim, same fallback to the published business
inbox, and the SMS state comes from alertConfig() itself rather than a
second copy of its rules, so this cannot disagree with what actually
happens.

The admin dashboard then says, above everything else and only when nothing
delivers: "Nothing tells anyone when a quote request arrives", names what
is unset, states that no error is raised, and — since DESK-01 already has
the numbers — how many are waiting and how long the oldest has waited. When
a channel IS configured it collapses to one quiet line naming the masked
destination and transport, so it can be verified rather than assumed. A
warning that is always on screen is furniture within a week.

NO SECRET LEAVES THE MODULE

The presence of a key is worth showing; its value is not. Keys, SIDs and
tokens are tested for emptiness and discarded — they never enter the
returned object — and destinations are masked, because an admin screen is a
page and pages get screenshotted. The test serialises the WHOLE output with
every credential populated and asserts that none of them appears anywhere in
it, rather than checking the fields somebody remembered to look at.

VERIFICATION

  42 assertions. Four mutations introduced, each caught: the auth token
  concatenated into a label, the destination rendered unmasked, whitespace
  treated as configuration, and the alarm made unconditional.

  Whitespace is in there because a variable set to " " in a hosting
  dashboard is the most likely way this reports live and delivers nothing.

  65 of 66 guards pass; verify:css cannot run in this sandbox (postcss
  unresolvable, npm 403) and fails identically on a clean checkout.
  parse-scan clean. delivery-health.ts typechecks with no errors of its own.

